Execute via full TDD cycle (red -> green -> commit). Last of 12 High-severity findings from an adversarial review. No plan@1/spec_file_path exists for the fix work itself, but this fix directly closes a gap already named in specs/SPEC-LUMEN-006-PATTERN.json's own reasoning text for CRIT-LUMEN-070 -- read that criterion's `reasoning` field first: it explicitly states "File-based redundancy detection (repeated reads of the same file) is a real, currently undetected gap -- not fixed here since it requires a lib.rs code change (checking target_symbol.or(target_file) rather than target_symbol alone), out of scope for a specs-only pass; tracked as a follow-up." This task IS that follow-up.

Workspace: /Users/gabe/Projects/lumen. Baseline: 13 fixes landed (cb29996..63445fc), `cargo test --workspace` green.

## The bug

crates/lumen-pattern/src/lib.rs: `TrajectoryGraph::push_tool` (around line 62-86) closes a cycle's back-edge using a combined key: `let target_key = node.target_symbol.clone().or_else(|| node.target_file.clone());` -- so a back-edge IS created whenever either the symbol OR the file matches a prior node. But `TrajectoryGraph::detect_circular_loops` (around line 88-107) only checks/reports using `target_symbol` alone:
```rust
let first_symbol = self.graph[component[0]].target_symbol.clone();
let is_redundant = component.iter().all(|idx| self.graph[*idx].target_symbol == first_symbol && !self.graph[*idx].is_mutation);
if is_redundant {
    if let Some(sym) = first_symbol {
        anomalies.push(CircularLoopAnomaly { symbol: sym, cycle_depth: component.len() });
    }
}
```
A Tarjan SCC component whose nodes are grounded on `target_file` ALONE (e.g. 3+ repeated `FileRead` calls on the same path -- `FileRead` grounds on `target_file`, never `target_symbol`, per `ground_tool_intent`'s own doc comment at line ~191-195) closes a real cycle in `push_tool` (back-edge created via the file-based key), but `first_symbol` is `None` for every node in that component, so `if let Some(sym) = first_symbol` at line 100 silently drops it -- no anomaly is ever reported, and `calculate_monotonicity` (which sums `cycle_depth` across `detect_circular_loops`'s output) reports a perfect `1.0` for a session stuck in a repeated-file-read loop.

## The fix

Extract the "grounded key" logic (symbol falling back to file) into one shared private associated function, and use it in BOTH `push_tool` (replacing its current inline duplicate of the same logic) AND `detect_circular_loops` (replacing its symbol-only check) -- single source of truth, eliminating the possibility of this specific drift recurring:

```rust
impl TrajectoryGraph {
    fn grounded_key(node: &ToolNode) -> Option<CompactString> {
        node.target_symbol.clone().or_else(|| node.target_file.clone())
    }
    // ... push_tool changes its `let target_key = node.target_symbol.clone().or_else(|| node.target_file.clone());`
    //     line to `let target_key = Self::grounded_key(&node);`
}
```

Then rewrite `detect_circular_loops` to use `Self::grounded_key(&self.graph[...])` instead of `.target_symbol.clone()` directly, both for `first_symbol` (rename to `first_key` if you like, your call) and the `is_redundant` all() check. The resulting `CircularLoopAnomaly.symbol` field ends up holding either a real symbol or a file path when that's what the cycle was grounded on -- this is the correct, intended behavior since `symbol` is the field that identifies what looped, and a file-path string is a perfectly valid value for it (check `CircularLoopAnomaly`'s doc comment / the spec's api_surface entry if you want to confirm this framing, but the field type is just `CompactString`, no semantic constraint preventing a file path).

